          %h2#relation_to_clifford_algebras Relation to Clifford algebras
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of
            = succeed "-geometric" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebras"} Clifford algebras
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            are equivalent. As a result, geometric algebras and Clifford algebras are isomorphic algebras, and the two terms are interchangeable with each other. In fact, Clifford originally named Clifford algebras “geometric algebras”, and it was later mathematicians who started using the term “Clifford algebra” for this object.
          %h3#in_physics In physics
          %p
            The use of the term “geometric algebra” rather than “Clifford algebra” for this object continues in physics in a school of thought following
            %a{:href => "#Hestenes66"} Hestenes 66
            that emphasizes the usefulness of making this abstract algebra explicit in the exposition of geometry and physics, such as on 2- and 3-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            and on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            In physics, geometric algebra may be seen as a third style of exposition and notation, in between 1) the traditional physics style of regarding the Clifford algebra in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+algebras"} tensor algebras
            by ideals defined by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+forms"} quadratic forms
          %p
            In textbooks on geometric algebra, the algebra is instead introduced without choosing matrix representations, but highlighting the obvious
            = succeed "-presentation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+algebras"} tensor algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hestenes"} David Hestenes
            reports to have experienced when he understood Clifford algebra in physics this way, back as a student, is what made geometric algebra become the school of thought that is today.
          %p
            One point being made here is that the traditional physics textbook emphasis on the special geometry of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            with its exceptional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+product"} vector product
            structure is outdated and contra-productive and can be replaced by a more elegant and more universal description in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivector"} bivector
            calculus (which canonically embeds into the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rotation"} rotation
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
